    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.08-07",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Rainfall also affects livestock indirectly through pasture growth and directly through drinking-water availability. A poor rainy season can reduce feed, weaken animals and require lower stocking or supplementary feed. Buying feed may help if it is affordable and available, but raises costs and does not restore degraded grazing by itself. A rainfall-to-livestock explanation should include the vegetation or water link rather than imply that animals produce less simply because rain is absent.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.2.08-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.08-2",
