    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.08-08",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Soil supplies rooting space, water and nutrients, with properties varying between locations. Depth influences how much soil can store water and support roots. Texture affects water movement and retention: very sandy soils may drain rapidly, while poorly structured fine soils may become waterlogged. Structure, organic matter and management can modify these effects. Texture alone should not be used as a complete judgement of fertility or crop suitability.",
      "claim_ids": [
