    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.08-15",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Household responsibilities can shape labour availability. Schooling, care work, illness or off-farm employment may compete with time needed for planting and harvesting. Cooperation or hired services can ease a bottleneck if accessible. The social explanation should describe the practical effect on an operation instead of making unsupported assumptions about a group's willingness to work. Different households within the same village may have different labour, knowledge and income resources.",
      "claim_ids": [
