    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.08-20",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "An input can be available in principle but inaccessible to a particular farmer. A reservoir may contain water while the farm has no lawful allocation or functioning delivery system. A tractor service may operate nearby while its booking arrives after the planting window. A buyer may exist but require a volume or quality the farm cannot supply alone. These examples distinguish regional availability from effective farm access. They also explain why public or cooperative services may need suitable timing and organisation to produce benefits.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.2.08-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.08-2",
        "CL-6137-2.2.08-01-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.08-01-2",
